WO-13: confirm ArduSub boots, arms, dives, holds depth, full thruster control.
Underwater equivalent of the surface boot verification. All depth figures are
cross-checked two independent ways: MAVLink (GLOBAL_POSITION_INT.relative_alt)
and TRUE Gazebo pose (z on /model/bluerov2/odometry, 100 Hz, independent of
anything ArduSub reports).

STACK (self-contained underwater boot; formalized later in WO-14):
  1. gz sim -s -r sim_config/underwater_world.sdf   (headless; spawns bluerov2)
     GZ_SIM_RESOURCE_PATH=sim_config/models:ardupilot_gazebo/models:SITL_Models
     GZ_SIM_SYSTEM_PLUGIN_PATH=ardupilot_gazebo/build
  2. MCR_VEHICLE_PROFILE=bluerov2 python3 attacks/gps_spoof.py  (relay, spoof OFF;
     stdin via a FIFO held open by a keepalive writer -- same trick as WO-10).
     Confirmed bound ('127.0.0.1', 9002) -> Gazebo ('127.0.0.1', 9100).
  3. build/sitl/bin/ardusub --model JSON --speedup 1 --slave 0
     --sim-address=127.0.0.1 -I0 --home -33.724223,150.679736,0.0,0.0
     --defaults <dave>/config/bluerov2/ardusub.parm   (direct binary; no MAVProxy)
  4. scratchpad/wo10/mav_bridge.py  (mirrors tcp:5760 -> UDP 14551/14552/14553).

  Operational note learned here: with the model's <lock_step>1</lock_step>,
  Gazebo advances only in step with ArduSub's FDM -- if ArduSub dies, Gazebo
  FREEZES (odometry stops, MAVLink heartbeat goes stale as sys 0). Long-running
  SITL/bridge must be launched so they persist for the whole session (the
  harness background-task mechanism), NOT as `nohup ... &` inside a one-shot
  shell whose process group is torn down on return -- that silently killed one
  ArduSub mid-test and froze the world. WO-14's run_sim.sh must own process
  lifetimes explicitly.

RESULTS (final verified run + targeted authority test):

  Boot:        world + relay + ArduSub + bridge all connected; ArduSub received
               FDM JSON (position/quaternion/velocity/imu), validated structures,
               loaded BlueROV2 defaults. Heartbeat sys 1.

  Arm:         MANUAL mode confirmed (custom_mode 19), ARMED confirmed via the
               heartbeat SAFETY_ARMED flag (retry-until-confirmed; a single
               unverified arm can silently fail, after which RC overrides are
               ignored and the vehicle only drifts passively -- caused several
               false "no thrust" readings until arming was verified).

  Dive:        MANUAL, RC ch3=1300 (throttle down) 12 s:
               Gazebo z -4.482 m -> -14.597 m  (descended 10.11 m)
               MAVLink rel_alt -0.782 m -> -11.340 m (descended 10.56 m)
               Both agree. Descent authority ~0.82 m/s.

  Hold depth:  ALT_HOLD engaged (custom_mode 2 CONFIRMED via drained heartbeat),
               sticks neutral, 15 s:  z -14.916 m -> -15.327 m, drift -0.412 m
               (~0.027 m/s). Holds depth within <0.5 m. (Contrast: before the
               buoyancy trim below, ALT_HOLD ran AWAY upward at +0.21 m/s.)

  Thruster
  authority:   Verified-armed, symmetric and strong in both vertical directions:
               ch3=1300 (down): -0.822 m/s ;  ch3=1700 (up): +0.819 m/s.
               => The AUV can surface under active thruster control at ~0.8 m/s,
               which is what WO-17 (GPS during surfaced windows) will rely on.

MODEL TRIM (WO-13 change to sim_config/models/bluerov2/model.sdf):
  base_link collision box height 0.065 -> 0.0632, so displaced mass
  (0.457*0.338*0.0632*1025 = 10.01 kg) ~ vehicle mass (10.0 kg + thruster/camera
  links) = near-neutral (a few grams negative). The original +0.29 kg positive
  buoyancy (~2.85 N up) overwhelmed ArduSub's default depth controller (ALT_HOLD
  climbed at 0.21 m/s). A real BlueROV2 is likewise ballasted to near-neutral for
  depth work, so this is a faithful trim. Trade-off noted: the vehicle no longer
  floats up passively on power loss (SURFACE mode / active up-thrust is used to
  surface instead) -- acceptable, and surfacing under thrust is verified above.

VERDICT: WO-13 PASS. ArduSub boots, arms, dives, holds depth, and has full
symmetric thruster authority on the BlueROV2 in the self-contained underwater
world. This is the first time the underwater (Track U) stack has run end-to-end
in this project. Underwater half of CHECKPOINT 1 is satisfied.
